### What is a late event in Flink?

A window fires when the watermark crosses its end time. Any event whose timestamp belongs to that window but arrives after the watermark has passed is late. This happens frequently in real systems where mobile networks, retries, or batch uploads introduce delays.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingEventTimeWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

public class LateEventDemo {
    // Window [10:00 - 10:01) fires when watermark >= 10:01:00
    // A transaction with POS time 10:00:30 arriving after
    // watermark 10:01:00 is LATE

    // stream.keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    //     .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    //     .sum("amount")
    // Late events are silently dropped by default
}
```

```text
Timeline:
  10:00:00  Window [10:00 - 10:01) opens
  10:00:30  Transaction T1 arrives (in window, processed)
  10:00:45  Transaction T2 arrives (in window, processed)
  10:01:00  Watermark reaches 10:01 --> window FIRES
  10:01:15  Transaction T3 (POS time 10:00:35) arrives
            T3 is LATE --> dropped by default
```

### What is the default behavior for late events?

By default Flink silently drops late events without any error or notification. The window's state is purged after firing, so there is nowhere to put the late record. This is efficient but loses data, which is unacceptable for correctness-sensitive financial workloads.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingEventTimeWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

public class DefaultDropDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(nbiWatermarkStrategy())
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .sum("amount") // late events SILENTLY DROPPED
            .print("Settlement");

        // Late transaction T3 at 10:00:35 arriving at 10:01:15
        // is dropped. Settlement total is WRONG.
        env.execute("NBI Default Drop Demo");
    }
}
```

### How does allowed lateness work?

With allowed lateness, the window does not purge its state immediately after firing. It keeps the state for the extra lateness period. During that grace period, every late event triggers the window function again and emits an updated result.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingEventTimeWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

public class AllowedLatenessDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(nbiWatermarkStrategy())
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(2)) // keep state 2 min extra
            .sum("amount")
            .print("Settlement");

        // Window [10:00-10:01) fires at watermark 10:01:00
        // Output: ACC-001 = Rs80,000 (first result)
        // Late T3 arrives at 10:01:15 (within 2-min grace)
        // Window re-fires: ACC-001 = Rs85,000 (corrected result)
        env.execute("NBI Allowed Lateness Demo");
    }
}
```

```text
Output timeline:
  [10:01:00] Settlement> ACC-001 = Rs80,000  (first fire)
  [10:01:15] Settlement> ACC-001 = Rs85,000  (re-fire with late T3)
  // Downstream sink sees TWO results for the same window!
  // Sink must be idempotent or upsert-capable
```

### How do side outputs capture very late events?

Events later than the allowed-lateness period are routed to a side output rather than being dropped. You define an OutputTag and attach it to the window, then retrieve the side stream for separate processing.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.DataStream;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.SingleOutputStreamOperator;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingEventTimeWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;
import org.apache.flink.util.OutputTag;

public class SideOutputLateDemo {
    // Tag for very-late events
    private static final OutputTag<Transaction> LATE_TAG =
        new OutputTag<Transaction>("late-transactions") {};

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        SingleOutputStreamOperator<String> mainStream =
            env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
                .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(nbiWatermarkStrategy())
                .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
                .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
                .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(2))
                .sideOutputLateData(LATE_TAG) // route very-late events
                .sum("amount");

        // Main output: normal + slightly-late corrected results
        mainStream.addSink(new SettlementSink());

        // Side output: very-late events for reconciliation
        DataStream<Transaction> lateStream =
            mainStream.getSideOutput(LATE_TAG);
        lateStream.addSink(new ReconciliationSink());

        env.execute("NBI Side Output Late Demo");
    }
}
```

### How do allowed lateness and side outputs work together?

The two tools combine in layers. A modest allowed lateness handles the common slightly-late case automatically with recomputation. A side output catches the rare very-late case for human or batch handling. This gives both low latency and no silent data loss.

```text
Layered late-event handling:

  Event arrives          Within window?     Within allowed     Side output?
                                            lateness?
  ──────────────         ──────────────     ──────────────     ──────────
  Before watermark       YES: processed     N/A                N/A
  After watermark,       NO                 YES: re-fire       N/A
    within grace                            window
  After watermark,       NO                 NO                 YES: routed
    beyond grace                                               to side output
  No side output         NO                 NO                 DROPPED
    configured
```

```java
// Complete layered setup for NBI settlement
stream
    .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(2))       // layer 1: recompute
    .sideOutputLateData(LATE_TAG)           // layer 2: capture
    .sum("amount");
```

### What are common mistakes with late event handling?

The most common mistake is not making the downstream sink idempotent when using allowed lateness. The window re-fires and emits a second result for the same window, so an append-only sink produces duplicates. Another mistake is setting allowed lateness too high, which keeps window state in memory for a long time and increases checkpoint size.

```java
// WRONG: append-only sink with allowed lateness
// Window fires twice for the same key+window
// Sink appends both: Rs80,000 AND Rs85,000 --> double-counted!
stream.window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(2))
    .sum("amount")
    .addSink(new AppendOnlyJdbcSink()); // WRONG: appends duplicates

// CORRECT: upsert sink that overwrites the previous result
stream.window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(2))
    .sum("amount")
    .addSink(new UpsertJdbcSink()); // CORRECT: overwrites with correction
```

### When should you configure allowed lateness?

Configure allowed lateness when you cannot afford to lose slightly-late data and your downstream sink can handle re-emissions. The right value is a business decision: how long do you expect common stragglers to arrive, balanced against how long you want to keep window state. For a banking settlement report, two minutes of allowed lateness covers mobile retries without excessive state retention.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

public class LatenessDecisionGuide {
    // Decision guide for NBI pipeline:
    //
    // Settlement report (must be accurate):
    //   allowedLateness = 2 minutes (covers mobile retries)
    //   sideOutput = yes (reconciliation for hour-late merchants)
    //   sink = upsert (handles re-emissions)
    //
    // Ops dashboard (approximate is fine):
    //   allowedLateness = 0 (default, drop late events)
    //   sideOutput = no
    //   sink = append (no corrections needed)
    //
    // Regulatory audit feed:
    //   allowedLateness = 5 minutes (strict compliance)
    //   sideOutput = yes (manual review for extreme latecomers)
    //   sink = upsert with audit trail
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know about late events?

A senior engineer should know that allowed lateness keeps window state in memory, increasing checkpoint size proportionally, that the re-emission pattern requires the entire downstream pipeline to be correction-aware, and that for extreme lateness (hours or days) a batch reconciliation job is more practical than keeping windows open.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class SeniorLateEventConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Senior-level: monitor late event counts via metrics
        // Flink exposes numLateRecordsDropped per operator
        // Alert if late drops exceed threshold

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(nbiWatermarkStrategy())
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(2))
            .sideOutputLateData(LATE_TAG)
            .aggregate(new SettlementAggregator())
            .addSink(new UpsertSettlementSink());

        // Side output: very-late events go to reconciliation
        // Batch job runs nightly to settle any remaining gaps
        env.execute("NBI Senior Late Event Config");
    }
}
```
